# Per-host colour ramps -- the one place they are defined.
#
# Everything that paints "which machine is this" reads this file: the starship
# prompt (sh/conf.d/60-starship.sh appends the chosen row to its generated
# config) and the tmux status bar (tmux.sh pushes it into @hp_* options).
# Which row a machine uses is decided by lib.sh; `host-palette` lists and
# switches them. Adding a row here is the only step needed to make a new
# palette selectable everywhere.
#
# One palette per line, whitespace separated, `#` starts a comment line:
#
#   c1  lightest -- the host pill, the brightest thing on screen
#   c2  vivid    -- starship's directory, tmux's current window
#   c3  dark     -- starship's git, tmux's session
#   c4  darker   -- starship's toolchain stop
#   c5  darkest  -- starship's status/duration stop, tmux's bar background
#   t1  near-black text for c1/c2, t2 light text for c3, t5 muted text for c5
#
# Most rows come from one HSL recipe (at H=222 it reproduces starship's
# tokyo-night preset, hence `blue`), so they read as the same design -- only
# the hue changes (S/L in percent):
#   c1 S35 L73   c2 S80 L70   c3 S27 L30   c4 S24 L17   c5 S25 L15
#   t1 S12 L5    t2 S26 L93   t5 S25 L71
# slate is the same recipe at roughly a third of the saturation; neon-cyan and
# light-green are hand-picked.
#
# name      c1      c2      c3      c4      c5      t1      t2      t5
